WEBVTT

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.812
The engine proposes two counteroffers.

00:00:02.812 --> 00:00:04.612
One offer is authorized.

00:00:04.612 --> 00:00:07.121
The other needs an underwriter’s review.

00:00:07.191 --> 00:00:11.481
How would you check that only the
authorized offer became a funded account?

00:00:11.741 --> 00:00:15.281
On Windows, PowerShell
starts the local console.

00:00:15.416 --> 00:00:19.066
The HTML form collects
the experiment settings.

00:00:19.109 --> 00:00:21.727
JavaScript sends them to Python,

00:00:21.837 --> 00:00:24.427
which checks the request
and starts a worker.

00:00:24.502 --> 00:00:26.962
That worker coordinates the stages.

00:00:27.011 --> 00:00:32.151
PostgreSQL runs the SQL rules
and records the results.

00:00:32.156 --> 00:00:34.906
Power BI and Tableau let us inspect them.

00:00:35.176 --> 00:00:36.926
The folders follow the work.

00:00:36.931 --> 00:00:40.601
Module 0 establishes the run
and its inputs.

00:00:40.645 --> 00:00:44.175
Module 1 builds the evidence
for each application.

00:00:44.353 --> 00:00:48.003
Module 2 takes that evidence
through underwriting,

00:00:48.003 --> 00:00:50.613
booking and repayment monitoring.

00:00:50.923 --> 00:00:53.893
Open the booking stage, Module 2.4.

00:00:53.935 --> 00:00:58.115
Its business requirement says funding
starts with an authorized offer.

00:00:58.246 --> 00:01:01.576
The SQL reads the previous
stage’s decision

00:01:01.617 --> 00:01:05.820
and sends a counteroffer awaiting review
down a separate path.

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:09.270
The stage test tries to put $1,000

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:12.420
on an inactive record
in a temporary table.

00:01:12.584 --> 00:01:14.654
The database rejects the row.

00:01:14.784 --> 00:01:17.724
That rejection is what earns a pass.

00:01:17.894 --> 00:01:21.380
You can inspect both the test
and the saved result.

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:25.800
I checked the decisions against the
funding records in the local campaign.

00:01:25.937 --> 00:01:29.677
750 merchants, two scenarios each.

00:01:29.723 --> 00:01:33.933
All 150 authorized decisions
led to funding.

00:01:34.259 --> 00:01:37.449
265 decisions went to review.

00:01:37.579 --> 00:01:39.479
None produced an advance.

00:01:39.699 --> 00:01:43.639
That tells me the engine honored
the funding rule in this run.

00:01:43.846 --> 00:01:45.316
Was the rule a good one?

00:01:45.441 --> 00:01:49.811
Next I’d compare referrals
with underwriters’ reasons,

00:01:50.026 --> 00:01:55.598
and funded offers with actual sales,
operating expenses and repayments.

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:58.770
Could merchants repay
and still make payroll?

